  "worked_solution": "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A\u2282{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={\u222c}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=[0,3]\\times [0,1],$ and the event $A={Y<X<Y+2}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14095567/assets/f4a0cc357eee835c84ef08524d27e3c30b98d58e1b6b5083b7df6a66ce987b6b.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<X<Y+2) & ={\u222c}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\u222c}_{A}\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\text{Area}(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
